1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:04,189
Why is ‘The furniture are expensive’
wrong when a room contains

2
00:00:04,229 --> 00:00:05,598
several chairs?

3
00:00:05,638 --> 00:00:09,835
And why do we say ‘much rice’
when rice has thousands of grains?

4
00:00:09,875 --> 00:00:12,614
A memorised list tells you the answer.

5
00:00:12,654 --> 00:00:14,472
It doesn’t show you why.

6
00:00:16,003 --> 00:00:17,732
In the meanings we are studying,

7
00:00:17,772 --> 00:00:20,575
English treats these nouns as uncountable,

8
00:00:20,615 --> 00:00:22,994
so they take a singular verb.

9
00:00:23,034 --> 00:00:25,951
Let’s see five pictures
behind the examples:

10
00:00:25,991 --> 00:00:29,895
an overall collection,
a material, a measured amount,

11
00:00:29,935 --> 00:00:32,577
a body of knowledge, and an activity.

12
00:00:34,103 --> 00:00:36,119
First: unit or mass.

13
00:00:36,159 --> 00:00:37,971
‘The furniture are expensive.’

14
00:00:38,011 --> 00:00:41,438
Look: a chair, a table, a sofa and a bed.

15
00:00:41,478 --> 00:00:43,373
I can count the separate objects:

16
00:00:43,413 --> 00:00:45,336
one chair, two chairs.

17
00:00:45,376 --> 00:00:47,004
But when I call them furniture,

18
00:00:47,044 --> 00:00:49,621
I’ve changed the way
I’m talking about them.

19
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:53,786
Furniture is the overall
label for the collection,

20
00:00:53,826 --> 00:00:56,440
not the name of each separate item.

21
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:59,189
English treats that word as uncountable.

22
00:00:59,229 --> 00:01:01,484
So ‘The furniture is expensive.’

23
00:01:01,524 --> 00:01:04,435
To give a number,
I count pieces of furniture:

24
00:01:04,475 --> 00:01:07,246
‘Two pieces of furniture are expensive.’

25
00:01:07,286 --> 00:01:09,172
Here pieces is plural.

26
00:01:11,103 --> 00:01:13,279
The same picture helps with luggage:

27
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:16,947
several bags and suitcases,
one luggage label.

28
00:01:16,987 --> 00:01:20,708
‘The luggage is heavy’,
but ‘three pieces of luggage are heavy’.

29
00:01:20,748 --> 00:01:24,605
You can picture clothing,
equipment and crockery similarly.

30
00:01:26,337 --> 00:01:28,299
Second: material.

31
00:01:28,339 --> 00:01:29,040
Think of gold.

32
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:32,634
A ring, a coin and a necklace
are three different things,

33
00:01:32,674 --> 00:01:36,004
but the gold is the substance
from which they are made.

34
00:01:36,044 --> 00:01:39,911
When the word names the substance,
I ask ‘How much gold?’

35
00:01:41,137 --> 00:01:44,982
I count three rings,
or measure twenty grams of gold.

36
00:01:45,022 --> 00:01:47,508
The number belongs to rings or grams,

37
00:01:47,548 --> 00:01:49,471
not directly to gold.

38
00:01:49,511 --> 00:01:51,885
Picture the same distinction with silver,

39
00:01:51,925 --> 00:01:55,695
wood, glass and cotton
when they name materials.

40
00:01:57,137 --> 00:02:00,195
There is an important correction
to the brick example in my

41
00:02:00,235 --> 00:02:03,510
notes: ‘This house is made
of bricks’ is correct.

42
00:02:03,550 --> 00:02:05,739
Those are individual blocks.

43
00:02:05,779 --> 00:02:09,564
‘This house is made of brick’
is also correct when brick means

44
00:02:09,604 --> 00:02:10,858
the material.

45
00:02:10,898 --> 00:02:14,671
The difference is what the word
names in that sentence.

46
00:02:16,603 --> 00:02:18,736
Third: a measured food amount.

47
00:02:18,776 --> 00:02:21,235
‘I ate too many rice for lunch’ is wrong.

48
00:02:21,275 --> 00:02:24,283
If we zoom in, we can see many grains.

49
00:02:24,323 --> 00:02:26,347
But when I say rice as food,

50
00:02:26,387 --> 00:02:28,892
I am talking about the amount in the bowl,

51
00:02:28,932 --> 00:02:31,353
not listing the grains one by one.

52
00:02:32,603 --> 00:02:35,013
So: ‘I ate too much rice for lunch.’

53
00:02:35,053 --> 00:02:36,550
If I want to count something,

54
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:40,468
I can say ‘many grains of rice’
or ‘two bowls of rice’.

55
00:02:40,508 --> 00:02:42,127
Watch where the number goes:

56
00:02:42,167 --> 00:02:44,354
onto grains or bowls.

57
00:02:44,394 --> 00:02:47,692
Bread works the same way
in ‘three slices of bread’;

58
00:02:47,732 --> 00:02:49,801
milk in ‘two litres of milk’.

59
00:02:51,537 --> 00:02:54,612
This food group overlaps with substances.

60
00:02:54,652 --> 00:02:58,290
The reason I have given it
its own picture is that a bowl

61
00:02:58,330 --> 00:03:02,281
of rice or a jug of milk makes
the idea of measuring an amount

62
00:03:02,321 --> 00:03:04,055
easy to see.

63
00:03:05,603 --> 00:03:07,218
Fourth: abstract.

64
00:03:07,258 --> 00:03:11,451
‘He has many knowledges about
art’ is wrong in ordinary English.

65
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:14,664
Here knowledge is his whole
body of understanding.

66
00:03:14,704 --> 00:03:16,628
It may contain many facts,

67
00:03:16,668 --> 00:03:20,617
but the word knowledge
is not naming each fact separately.

68
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:24,428
Say, ‘He has a lot
of knowledge about art.’

69
00:03:24,468 --> 00:03:27,020
Think also of advice and information:

70
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,852
‘some advice’, ‘useful information’.

71
00:03:29,892 --> 00:03:33,275
If I need a number,
I can say ‘two pieces of advice’,

72
00:03:33,315 --> 00:03:37,004
‘three pieces of information’,
or count ‘three facts’.

73
00:03:38,737 --> 00:03:41,498
Abstract is a picture that helps us think,

74
00:03:41,538 --> 00:03:44,639
but it cannot decide every case by itself.

75
00:03:44,679 --> 00:03:48,855
An idea is abstract,
yet ‘three ideas’ is correct.

76
00:03:48,895 --> 00:03:52,018
We have to notice whether
this particular word names

77
00:03:52,058 --> 00:03:56,103
an undivided body or separate
items in the sentence.

78
00:03:57,637 --> 00:04:00,098
Fifth: activity or process.

79
00:04:00,138 --> 00:04:04,169
‘She does many works every day’
is wrong when I mean her daily effort.

80
00:04:04,209 --> 00:04:07,181
She might type, plan, call and clean:

81
00:04:07,221 --> 00:04:08,735
several actions.

82
00:04:08,775 --> 00:04:11,835
But work names that effort
as an ongoing whole.

83
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:15,541
So: ‘She does a lot of work every day.’

84
00:04:15,581 --> 00:04:18,478
To count the actions,
I choose another noun:

85
00:04:18,518 --> 00:04:21,360
‘She completes four tasks every day.’

86
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,757
Count the tasks;

87
00:04:22,797 --> 00:04:25,828
describe the work
as an amount of activity.

88
00:04:27,570 --> 00:04:29,813
But ‘three works of art’ is correct:

89
00:04:29,853 --> 00:04:32,939
in a gallery, works means created pieces.

90
00:04:32,979 --> 00:04:34,784
The meaning has changed.

91
00:04:36,337 --> 00:04:40,109
Now try three new sentences.

92
00:04:40,149 --> 00:04:41,412
‘The luggage is heavy’:

93
00:04:41,452 --> 00:04:45,480
several bags, one overall label.

94
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,058
‘I need much more rice’:

95
00:04:47,098 --> 00:04:50,310
the food as an amount.

96
00:04:50,350 --> 00:04:51,943
‘Her advice is useful’:

97
00:04:51,983 --> 00:04:55,584
advice as information,
not separate suggestions.

98
00:04:55,624 --> 00:04:58,546
Each time, picture what the noun
is naming before you choose

99
00:04:58,586 --> 00:04:59,871
the grammar.

100
00:05:01,603 --> 00:05:03,307
These are the five pictures:

101
00:05:03,347 --> 00:05:05,857
collection, material, measured amount,

102
00:05:05,897 --> 00:05:07,896
body of knowledge, process.

103
00:05:07,936 --> 00:05:09,640
See the meaning first.

104
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:11,926
Then ask what you can count directly,

105
00:05:11,966 --> 00:05:13,702
or which unit you need.

106
00:05:13,742 --> 00:05:16,559
That is how the correction
becomes understandable instead

107
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:19,065
of something you have to recite.

